## Behaviour and content

The exclamation mark is decorative; assistive technology receives the word “Warning” as part of the message. The text names the actual consequence. Avoid all-capital instructions and vague phrases such as “Are you sure?” without context.

Use a [confirmation or interruption pattern](../patterns/interruption.html) when the decision needs more explanation. Keep the cancel route clear. Do not make destructive actions look like the ordinary positive primary action simply because the selected palette is green or red.

## Accessibility and testing

Check that the message appears before the action in both visual and reading order. Test high zoom, screen-reader output and forced colours. Verify the real action’s permission checks, confirmation and failure handling separately; this component supplies only the warning presentation.

## Source and implementation boundary

[GOV.UK Warning text](https://design-system.service.gov.uk/components/warning-text/) supplies the visual structure. The consequences above are explicitly example content. The theme preserves existing HA confirmations and must not infer new device capabilities.
